{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Arizona Diamondbacks opened the season like a team on a mission, tearing through April with an 18-7 record that made them look like genuine NL West contenders. Paul Goldschmidt was mashing, the rotation was holding up, and even the bullpen — which would prove to be a persistent headache — had its moments. That blazing start gave the desert faithful every reason to believe this was the year Arizona would return to October baseball.\n\nThen May arrived, and everything fell apart. The D-backs went 8-19, the worst month of their season by a wide margin, and the bullpen became the story for all the wrong reasons. Brad Boxberger, entrusted with high-leverage situations, became a recurring villain in his own team's highlight reel — surrendering crushing home runs that flipped games and sapped momentum. The rotation also wobbled, and what had looked like a division contender suddenly looked fragile.\n\nJune and July told a more complicated story. The team bounced back to go 19-9 in June, riding clutch moments and some timely opponent errors to claw their way back into relevance. July was a wash at 13-13, a month of treading water as the bullpen continued to blow leads and Paul Goldschmidt's heroics often came in losing efforts. August offered a modest winning record but no real surge, and by the time September arrived, the window had closed — an 8-19 collapse to end the year mirrored the disastrous May and sealed their fate outside the playoffs.\n\nThe 2018 Diamondbacks were a team of genuine talent undone by inconsistency and a leaky bullpen. Goldschmidt delivered a monster season, Robbie Ray and Zack Greinke provided rotation quality, and David Peralta emerged as a legitimate offensive force. But an 82-80 record told the honest story: a team that could be brilliant and could be maddening, sometimes within the same game, and ultimately came up just short of earning another October chance.",
